lvim-preview — vendored browser render assets
=============================================

These are third-party BROWSER runtime assets (JS/CSS/fonts), pinned to exact
versions and served locally by the in-plugin HTTP server so the preview page
makes NO external/CDN request ever (fully offline). They are runtime assets,
not Lua dependencies. Each keeps its upstream LICENSE beside it; health.lua
(:checkhealth lvim-preview) verifies every asset below is present.

Do not hand-edit the minified files. To refresh, re-fetch the exact version
from the source below and update this manifest.

asset             | version  | license      | source
------------------|----------|--------------|---------------------------------------------------------
katex             | 0.16.11  | MIT          | npm katex                  → dist/katex.min.{js,css}, dist/contrib/{auto-render,mhchem}.min.js, dist/fonts/*.woff2
mermaid           | 11.4.1   | MIT          | npm mermaid                → dist/mermaid.min.js
highlight.js      | 11.11.1  | BSD-3-Clause | cdnjs highlight.js 11.11.1 → highlight.min.js; npm highlight.js → styles/github{,-dark}.min.css
github-markdown-css | 5.8.1  | MIT          | npm github-markdown-css    → github-markdown.css
pdf.js            | 5.6.205  | Apache-2.0   | npm pdfjs-dist             → build/pdf.min.mjs, build/pdf.worker.min.mjs, standard_fonts/, cmaps/, wasm/

Every asset above is permissively licensed (MIT / BSD-3-Clause / Apache-2.0), which matches this
plugin's own BSD-3-Clause. Nothing here is copyleft, and adding something that is needs an
explicit decision, not a vendoring commit.

There is NO markdown or org renderer in this directory. Both are parsed and rendered to HTML by
our own Lua, in `lua/lvim-preview/markdown/` and `lua/lvim-preview/org/` — neither is a browser
asset at all, and that is why this plugin ships no markdown or org licence.

Notes
-----
* katex: only the .woff2 fonts are vendored (the modern format KaTeX lists
  first); ancient-browser .woff/.ttf fallbacks are omitted. katex.min.css
  references the fonts by the relative path fonts/… , which resolves under the
  served /@lvim-preview/vendor/katex/ prefix. The mhchem contrib is loaded only
  when features.katex_mhchem = true.
* highlight.js: the browser bundle is the common-languages cdnjs build; the two
  github themes are toggled by prefers-color-scheme when the preview theme is a
  static one, and are overridden by the palette when theme = "lvim".
* line numbering for sync-scroll (data-source-line anchors) is emitted by our own
  markdown and org HTML renderers, which know every node's source line exactly.
* pdf.js is ESM-only upstream (.mjs). It is bootstrapped by an inline
  `type="module"` block in template.pdf_shell, which is OUR code — no upstream
  file is modified. Four data directories from the SAME pdfjs-dist version are
  vendored and served under `/@lvim-preview/vendor/pdfjs/`, each loaded strictly
  ON DEMAND (a plain-Latin PDF pulls none of them, so the common case downloads
  nothing extra), because pdf.js would otherwise reach for a CDN and break the
  offline guarantee:
    - `standard_fonts/` — the 14 standard PDF fonts (a font not embedded in the
      document). Wired via `standardFontDataUrl`.
    - `cmaps/`  (~1.7 MB, 169 packed `.bcmap` files) — the CJK character maps; a
      CID-keyed CJK font needs the specific map for its encoding. Wired via
      `cMapUrl` + `cMapPacked: true`. Without them CJK text renders as tofu.
    - `wasm/`   (~0.9 MB) — the WebAssembly image decoders and colour transform:
      `openjpeg.wasm` (JPEG-2000 / JPX), `jbig2.wasm` (JBIG2), `qcms_bg.wasm`
      (ICC colour), plus `openjpeg_nowasm_fallback.js`. Wired via `wasmUrl`.
      Each carries its own upstream LICENSE_* beside it (Apache-2.0 / BSD /
      MIT-style, all permissive). Without them a JPEG-2000 image drops.
  The URL option names are those of pdf.js 5.6.205 (`cMapUrl`, `wasmUrl`,
  `standardFontDataUrl`); they have changed across major versions, so the cmaps
  and wasm set MUST match the vendored `pdf.min.mjs` version exactly — a cmap
  set from a different pdf.js is a silent renderer break.
* org has no entry here BY DESIGN. The only two JS org parsers with usable
  coverage are GPL-3.0-or-later (uniorg) or too weak to accept (orgajs, MIT,
  drops TODO keywords and emits <td> for table headers), so the org path was
  written in Lua instead and runs in Neovim: `lua/lvim-preview/org/`. The page
  receives finished HTML and loads no org library at all.
* markdown had a vendored renderer (markdown-it 14.1.0 + markdown-it-emoji 3.0.0)
  until it was replaced by our own Lua CommonMark parser in
  `lua/lvim-preview/markdown/`. The replacement was made on measurement, not
  preference: 644/652 of the CommonMark 0.31.2 conformance examples byte-exact
  (the 8 others differ only in whether a character reference is written out or
  decoded, which a browser resolves identically), and byte-identical HTML to
  markdown-it on 80 of 83 real markdown files in this ecosystem — the other 3
  differing only because markdown-it's fuzzy autolinker turned `setup.py`,
  `noxfile.py`, `manage.py` and `CLAUDE.md` into `http://` links. Emoji
  shortcodes (`:tada:`) went with markdown-it-emoji and are not implemented.
